WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.500
- By now, I think everyone has heard

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.230
of what happened in Uvalde, Texas.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.930
I'm not gonna go into detail,

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.100
and new facts are coming
to light every single day.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.500
But one thing it seems is
certain is that the police,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.800
to put a charitably,

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.640
did not act in a way that most
people wanted them to act.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.473
By all accounts,

00:00:19.473 --> 00:00:20.520
there was a very long delay

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.830
between the time that the
police arrived on scene

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.420
and when they took action
to stop the shooter.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.277
So, people angry at
the tragedy are asking,

00:00:28.277 --> 00:00:30.760
"Can the police be held
legally responsible,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.060
not for their action,
but for their inaction?"

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.590
And I'm not gonna hide
the ball on this one,

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.870
the answer is almost certainly no.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.750
But the reason why might surprise you,

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.820
because most people believe

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.940
that the police have a legal duty to help.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.500
And unfortunately, according
to the Supreme Court,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.780
that is not the case.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.900
While their motto may
be to protect and serve,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.040
the police have absolutely
no duty whatsoever

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.120
to help anyone that's in trouble.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.270
Now, normally, this would
be the part of the video

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.870
where I run through the factual summary

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.150
that gives rise to all
of the legal issues.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.300
I'm not gonna do that today

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.160
because, honestly, it's just too hard.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.310
But these are important legal issues.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:13.370
And this is one of those videos

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.030
where it's not about
what the law should be,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.820
it's about what the law is.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.090
Now, we've talked a lot on this channel

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:20.550
about the difficulty in suing police

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.790
for the things that they do,

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.610
the mistakes they might have made.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.640
Qualified immunity generally
protects police officers,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.640
unless it was clearly established

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.200
that what they did was unconstitutional.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.040
There are all kinds of
controversies in this doctrine.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.240
This is not the video for
those particular questions.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.170
But the question presented

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.220
by the Uvalde shooting is different.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.210
Here, we're asking whether
government officials

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.100
can be sued for actions they did not take.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.080
Now, the Supreme Court has ruled twice

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.750
that government actors like
police and social workers

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.770
actually have no duty to protect anyone.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.980
This precedent was established in 1989

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.020
when the Supreme Court
decided the case of DeShaney

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.240
versus Winnebago County
Department of Social Services.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.970
In that case, in 1982,

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.340
Joshua DeShaney was a four year old boy

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.500
living with his father,
Randy, in Wisconsin.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.150
Randy had primary custody of the child

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.610
and had recently gone
through a second divorce.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.670
Joshua's stepmother

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.730
told the Winnebago County
Department of Social Services

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.420
that Randy abused his son.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:17.253
Randy denied it,

00:02:17.253 --> 00:02:19.390
and the county took no
action to remove the child.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:23.460
However, in 1983, Joshua was
seen at the ER for bruises.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.430
The ER doctor notified social services,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.660
and Joshua was removed from
Randy's custody for a few days.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.620
Randy wasn't charged,

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.700
and the child was returned to his custody.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.390
A social worker was
assigned to visit the home.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.460
And in 1984, the social worker
visited the house 20 times,

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.110
noting that the child had bumps, bruises,

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.860
and injuries on his head.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.510
The social worker also documented

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.940
that Randy failed to attend
his regular counseling sessions

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.030
or enrolled Joshua in preschool.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:47.930
And during this time,

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.560
Joshua ended up in the ER
twice with suspicious injuries.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.680
The social worker said that
she feared for his life

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.090
but took no action to
remove him from the home.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.290
And unfortunately, in March of 1984,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.830
one day after the social worker's visit,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.780
Randy beat Joshua so hard
that he fell into a coma.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.400
Joshua had severe brain damage

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.360
and was permanently paralyzed.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.930
The state then placed Joshua

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.550
in an institution where he would remain

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.010
for the rest of his life.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.150
Joshua DeShaney's mother

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.840
sued the Winnebago County
Department of Social Services,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.900
alleging that the department
had deprived Joshua

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.030
of his liberty interest
in bodily integrity

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.400
in violation of his rights

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.830
under the substantive component

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.040
of the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.910
by failing to intervene to protect him

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.940
against his father's violence.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.980
Now, the Due Process Clause
of the 14th Amendment

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.990
declares that no state shall
deprive any person of life,

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.476
liberty, or property
without due process of law.

00:03:36.476 --> 00:03:38.310
DeShaney argued that the county

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.050
had a responsibility to protect Joshua

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.330
because it knew Randy abused him.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.920
The social worker had tracked the abuse

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.590
on paper for over a year.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:49.070
DeShaney argued that through
the social worker's visits,

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.330
the state had established
a special relationship

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.677
with Joshua that created a responsibility

00:03:53.677 --> 00:03:56.230
to protect him from known dangers.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.690
Then, chief justice of the
Supreme Court, William Rehnquist,

00:03:58.690 --> 00:03:59.740
wrote the majority opinion,

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.460
which found that the Due Process Clause

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.350
only applies to states,
not to private citizens.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.690
Since Randy was the one who beat his son,

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:07.523
the court reason

00:04:07.523 --> 00:04:10.550
that the state didn't directly
cause Joshua's injuries.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.450
And the majority also
rejected the argument

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.200
that the state made
Joshua more vulnerable.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.327
The purpose of the Due
Process Clause, quote,

00:04:17.327 --> 00:04:19.410
"was to protect people from the state,

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.050
not to ensure that the
state protected them

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:22.320
from each other."

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.790
The court held that the 14th Amendment

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.667
did not grant any citizen
a guarantee of, quote,

00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:28.260
"minimal levels of safety and security."

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.093
The court also said

00:04:29.093 --> 00:04:31.570
that people have no right
to governmental aid.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.420
The court held that the Due Process Clause

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.470
would only apply if the
state had assumed custody

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.050
of a person against their will,

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.470
and then failed to
provide for their safety

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:40.960
and general wellbeing.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.810
Justice Brennan and the
two other dissenters

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.643
saw it another way.

00:04:43.643 --> 00:04:45.470
They believed that since
the state had acted

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.470
through the social work department,

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.400
they assumed the responsibility
for the child's safety.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.340
The dissenters said that if
the state had never intervened,

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.550
then others in Joshua's
life, like his mother,

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:58.850
might have taken other actions
to get him away from Randy.

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:00.150
And basically, in the DeShaney case,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.300
what happens is that a mother
is worried that her son,

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.601
who's in the custody of the father,

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:07.570
is being abused by the father.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:11.470
She goes to the police, they
send social workers out,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:13.570
social worker visits 20 times,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.580
sees lots and lots of evidence of abuse.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.150
Ultimately, the social
services doesn't do enough,

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:23.130
and the child is horribly
and permanently injured

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.170
by the father in large part

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:29.950
because social services allowed the child

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.030
to remain with the husband.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:33.410
Now, there are obvious differences

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.210
between what happened in Uvalde

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.590
and a social worker who fails

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:41.020
to remove a child from a violent parent.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.860
But that takes us to the 2005 case

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.740
of the town of Castle Rock,
Colorado versus Gonzales,

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.050
where the Supreme Court
applied the same rationale

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.370
to a case involving the police.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:52.203
Now, in that case,

00:05:52.203 --> 00:05:54.260
Jessica Gonzales had asked
for a restraining order

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.050
against her estranged husband.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:57.020
There was a Colorado law

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.040
that made domestic violence
restraining orders mandatory

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.250
whenever one party felt threatened.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.210
And the state trial court issued an order

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.730
prohibiting the husband
from seeing Ms. Gonzales

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:06.830
or their three daughters,

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.540
unless it was through
a pre-arranged visit.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.960
Now, just one month later,

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.140
the husband kidnapped the three children.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.610
Ms. Gonzales had direct
evidence that he had the kids

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.340
because he confirmed it on the phone.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.060
She begged the police to search
for her husband right away

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.190
since she violated the court order.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.030
Now, the Castle Rock Police Department

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.110
told Ms. Gonzales that
it could do nothing,

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:28.320
and told her she had
to wait until 10:00 pm

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.020
to see if her husband
brought the daughters home.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:34.040
At 10:00 pm, the police told
Jessica to wait until midnight.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.450
At midnight, she went
to the police station

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.160
to beg them to arrest her husband.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.870
An officer took the report
and then went to dinner.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:40.703
A few hours later,

00:06:40.703 --> 00:06:42.290
Gonzales's husband went
to the police station

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:43.620
and opened fire.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.450
Police killed him in the melee.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:46.710
And when they searched his truck,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.370
they found the three
girls dead in the back.

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:52.410
Gonzales's husband had
murdered all three that night.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.400
Ms. Gonzales sued the Castle
Rock Police Department

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.470
for violating her due process rights.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:58.080
And the Tenth Circuit Court of Appeals

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.530
found that she had a, quote,
"protected property interest

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.190
in the enforcement of the terms
of her restraining order,"

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.890
which the police had violated.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.060
But the Supreme Court
reversed that decision,

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.170
ruling that Gonzales

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:10.410
had no constitutionally
protected property interest

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.240
in the enforcement of
the restraining order,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.073
and, therefore, could not claim

00:07:13.073 --> 00:07:16.310
that the police had violated
her right to due process.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.690
Justice Antonin Scalia
wrote the majority opinion.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:21.820
He reasoned that the state
law didn't entitle anyone

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.280
to mandatory action by police,

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:24.770
even though the issue
into the restraining order

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.250
was mandatory under Colorado law.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.555
Restraining orders provide the grounds

00:07:28.555 --> 00:07:32.060
for arresting a person
constrained to the order,

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:33.700
but that doesn't mean that the recipient

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.040
can force the police to act.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:37.920
Scalia wrote that under the Colorado law,

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.360
police have discretion to decide

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.960
whether to enforce an order,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.450
and if so, how they would do so.

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:44.567
The court concluded that, quote,

00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:46.170
"since Colorado had not created

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.700
such an entitlement to mandatory action,

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:50.440
Gonzales had no property interest,

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.960
and the Due Process Clause
was therefore inapplicable."

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.170
The court ultimately concluded

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.547
that unless a legislature
specifically said,

00:07:56.547 --> 00:07:58.930
"We're creating this
right, and if we breach it,

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.250
the private citizen can
then sue the government,"

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.380
then the citizen is out of luck.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:05.127
And as one court put it, quote,

00:08:05.127 --> 00:08:07.500
"Law enforcement officers
often consider themselves

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:08.910
responsible for protecting the public

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.430
and deterring criminal activity.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:12.518
However, the legal doctrine that specifies

00:08:12.518 --> 00:08:14.700
the legal duty of law enforcement officers

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.840
holds that they have a duty to the public,

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.370
but not to individual citizens."

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:22.180
This is what's known as
the public duty doctrine.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:23.570
Doctrine means that the officers

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.100
don't have a duty to protect citizens

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:26.910
from harm by a third party.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.050
In other words, there is no clear duty

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.250
to protect individual citizens.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:32.083
Now, that being said,

00:08:32.083 --> 00:08:34.810
the DeShaney decision recognized
two possible exceptions

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:36.510
to the general rule that
the state has no duty

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.150
to protect people from harm.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:38.983
The first is what's called

00:08:38.983 --> 00:08:40.490
the special relationship exception,

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:42.340
which holds that the constitution imposes

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:43.410
upon the state a duty of care

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:45.870
towards individuals
who are in its custody.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.217
As it's usually summarized, quote,

00:08:47.217 --> 00:08:49.420
"When the state takes a
person into its custody

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:51.310
and holds him there against his will,

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:52.531
the constitution imposes upon it

00:08:52.531 --> 00:08:55.670
a corresponding duty to
assume some responsibility

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:57.320
for his safety and well-being."

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:00.210
So when the state has a special
relationship with someone,

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.730
there may be a duty to protect.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:03.220
If this relationship exists,

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:04.920
the state official must
take reasonable steps

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.100
to ensure the safety
and care of that person

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.820
and to safeguard them
against foreseeable risks.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:11.500
However, most courts
say that the only time

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:12.980
the state forms a special relationship

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:15.027
is through incarceration,
institutionalization,

00:09:15.027 --> 00:09:18.530
or other similar restraint
of personal liberty.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:19.950
And most courts reject the theory

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:21.430
that people waiting to be rescued

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:23.420
are in police custody and control.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.160
This is particularly true
in the Fifth Circuit,

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:27.080
and perhaps the most
analogous Fifth Circuit case

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:28.610
is Salas versus Carpenter,

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:30.400
where a county clerk was taken hostage

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.430
at the courthouse by
her abusive boyfriend.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.140
And during the hostage negotiation,

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.840
the sheriff ordered that the city SWAT

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.640
and Hostage Negotiation
Teams leave the courthouse.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.640
He then replaced them
with county personnel,

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.440
not trained for SWAT or
hostage negotiations.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.270
The man killed the county clerk,

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:47.850
and the Fifth Circuit
sided with the sheriff,

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.490
finding that he did not violate
her constitutional rights

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:52.410
by using untrained negotiators.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:53.397
The court said, quote,

00:09:53.397 --> 00:09:55.410
"Generally, the absence
of a special relationship

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.069
defeats any due process
claim based on a failure

00:09:58.069 --> 00:10:00.180
to provide protective services.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.820
The victim was not held in state custody

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:05.340
or otherwise prevented by the
state from caring for herself.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.180
This was a failed rescue effort."

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:10.130
And it's easy to see
how the Fifth Circuit,

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:12.710
which covers Texas, would
probably make the same call

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.750
in the hypothetical Uvalde case.

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:16.570
The police were doing something,

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:18.110
they were securing the parking lot,

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:19.512
and stopping parents from getting inside

00:10:19.512 --> 00:10:21.490
where they might get hurt.

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:24.790
And just because the effort
failed and failed badly,

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.780
probably wouldn't change the
Fifth Circuit's analysis.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.530
But under the DeShaney case,
there is a second exception,

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:33.200
and that is called the
state-created danger exception.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.500
And under that theory,
some courts have recognized

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.590
that state officials have a
duty to protect individuals

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:39.690
from harm when their actions created

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.560
or exacerbated a danger
to that individual.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.570
But here, it seems like this
exception wouldn't apply.

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:47.590
The police didn't help,
at least not in the way

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:49.374
that most people wanted them to help,

00:10:49.374 --> 00:10:50.510
but, at the same time,

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:53.740
they also did not create
the situation itself.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.500
Now, those of you that
have been paying attention,

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.380
might have come up with a legal theory

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.910
that might differentiate what
happened to Uvalde, Texas

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:01.540
from the prior precedent.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:06.230
And that is that children
are mandated to go to school.

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:09.670
Often, those schools are
provided by the government.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:13.650
And in this case, this massacre
occurred at a public school.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:15.910
So aren't the children within
the custody of the government,

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.100
and doesn't the government
then have an obligation

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:20.660
to provide for the health
and safety of the children,

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:23.280
especially a school that
has a police officer,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:26.560
and a school district that
has its own police department?

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.150
Well, it's complicated,

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:29.921
but it probably doesn't turn out the way

00:11:29.921 --> 00:11:31.790
that you might want,

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:33.690
because, unfortunately,
this is not the first time

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:36.820
there has been a massacre
on school campus.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:40.790
And a relatively recent case
is almost directly on subject.

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:44.800
And that is the case arising
out of the Parkland situation.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.390
In late 2020, the Eleventh
Circuit Court of Appeals

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.600
sided the Castle Rock and DeShaney cases,

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:51.090
and dismissed a lawsuit brought

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.020
by the victims of the
Parkland, Florida shooting

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:55.410
at the Marjory Stoneman
Douglas High School.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:57.310
Now, there, the students
alleged that the county sheriff

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:59.450
and deputies incompetently responded

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:01.050
to an active shooter situation

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:03.090
violating their due process rights.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:04.810
The deputies knew that the shooter

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:07.510
was a danger to the community,

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:09.290
and school officials had also known

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:12.130
that the shooter was deeply
troubled and had made threats.

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:14.285
The plaintiffs alleged that
the deputies had failed

00:12:14.285 --> 00:12:16.050
to intervene during the shooting.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.820
And they also noted that
one deputy prevented EMS

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:20.510
from providing lifesaving care

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:22.640
and blocking them from entering the school

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:23.550
during the shooting.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:24.760
The Eleventh Circuit Court of Appeals,

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.987
which is one step below the
Supreme Court, found that,

00:12:26.987 --> 00:12:29.240
"A plaintiff can only allege a
substantive due process claim

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:30.100
in two situations.

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.580
First, when the person was
in the government's custody

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:35.930
and second, when government
official's conduct is arbitrary

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:37.790
or shocks the conscience."

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:38.623
Now, first,

00:12:38.623 --> 00:12:40.650
the court held that the children
were not in police custody,

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:42.280
and as far as shocking the conscience,

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.455
the court held that in an
active shooter situation,

00:12:44.455 --> 00:12:47.210
police have to make split-second decisions

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:48.600
in the midst of chaos.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:49.537
In this situation, quote,

00:12:49.537 --> 00:12:51.570
"An official's conduct
will shock the conscience

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:55.710
only when it stems from a,
quote, 'purpose to cause harm.'"

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:57.870
That means that a plaintiff
can only sue if they can show

00:12:57.870 --> 00:12:59.886
that the government officer
purposefully caused harm.

00:12:59.886 --> 00:13:02.720
If the police officer didn't
intend to hurt anyone,

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:04.200
then they can't be sued.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:05.033
And with respect

00:13:05.033 --> 00:13:07.870
to whether the children
were in government custody,

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:09.047
the court held, quote,

00:13:09.047 --> 00:13:11.050
"It is well-established
that school children

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:13.950
are not in a custodial
relationship with the state.

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.320
A custodial relationship exists only

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:17.860
if the government places limitations

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.640
upon individual's ability to act

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:22.484
on his own behalf that are similar in kind

00:13:22.484 --> 00:13:26.500
to incarceration or other forms
of involuntary confinement."

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.692
School attendance, even
when compelled by truancy

00:13:28.692 --> 00:13:31.330
and compulsory attendance laws,

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:33.630
is not similar in kind to incarceration

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:35.317
or institutional confinement.

00:13:35.317 --> 00:13:37.700
"Ordinarily, there are no
custodial relationships

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:38.980
in the public school system,

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:41.370
even if officials are
aware of potential dangers

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.160
or have expressed an intent to provide aid

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.920
on school grounds."

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:47.020
And in the Parkland case,

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:49.042
the biggest controversy
was about Scot Peterson,

00:13:49.042 --> 00:13:50.880
a Broward County Sheriff's Deputy

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.460
and school resource officer.

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:54.480
Peterson stayed outside
of a school building

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:55.490
for over 40 minutes

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:58.210
as students and faculty
members were gunned down.

00:13:58.210 --> 00:13:59.880
And the state of Florida charged Peterson

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:03.070
with neglect under a statute
that applies to caregivers.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:05.250
Now, this is generally a novel
interpretation of the law

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:06.772
which usually applies to parents

00:14:06.772 --> 00:14:10.290
or adult household members or teachers.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:13.500
Now, Peterson will stand
trial on this criminal case,

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.300
but the Eleventh Circuit
dismissed the civil case

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:16.980
against Peterson for the same reasons

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.780
it dismissed the cases
against other defendants,

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:20.958
that the students were not

00:14:20.958 --> 00:14:23.700
in a custodial relationship
with the officials,

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:26.390
even the school's armed resource officer.

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:29.620
A lot of people disagree
with this particular ruling,

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:33.030
and it can be solved with legislation,

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:34.680
but as it currently stands,

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.799
there is no inherent cause of action

00:14:36.799 --> 00:14:40.540
based on the constitution
that would allow parents

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:43.610
and teachers and victims to sue the police

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:45.310
for inaction at Uvalde.

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:48.030
And unfortunately, that is
the current state of the law.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.610
Now, if you'd like to
actually do some good,

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:51.487
one thing I can recommend

